# The sdist is the reproducible source snapshot: the product (`src/`, `accel/`)
# plus the track that proves it works (`tests/`, `docs/`). `accel/` has to be
# here, because an sdist build compiles it when a Rust toolchain is present.
#
# The `prune` lines below are load-bearing rather than decorative, and the
# reason changed with setuptools-scm. Its file finder offers every git-tracked
# file to the sdist, so this file stopped being an allowlist and became a
# blocklist over everything git knows about. Measured: with no prune for it,
# `.vscode/settings.json` reached the sdist.
#
# Note that `lab/` is only *mostly* ignored: `lab/README.md` and
# `lab/.gitignore` are tracked, so the finder offers them and the prune is what
# stops them. The rest of `lab/` is scratch work that no commit can reproduce,
# and patchcraft-0.2.0.tar.gz shipped three such files to PyPI.
#
# Adding a tracked directory to this repository ships it to PyPI by default.
# `tools/check_dist.py` holds the matching allowlist and will fail the release
# for anything unexpected, which is the reminder to come back here.

include LICENSE CITATION.cff
include CHANGELOG.md CONTRIBUTING.md
include README.md README.pt-BR.md README.pypi.md
include pyproject.toml setup.py uv.lock .python-version

recursive-include src *.py
include src/patchcraft/py.typed
recursive-include tests *.py
recursive-include docs *.md
recursive-include accel *.rs *.toml *.lock *.md
prune accel/target
recursive-include tools *.py

prune lab
prune archive
prune .github
prune .vscode
# Presentation material, not source. It is tracked, so the file finder offers
# it and only this line keeps it out.
prune outreach
